# art-review/ is deliberately NOT ignored, despite what
# .planning/art-generation-process.md §6 says. Gemini output is not reproducible —
# the same prompt gives a different image — so the repo is the only backup of
# art that cannot be remade, and ignored files are one `git clean -xdf` from
# gone. Revisit when the tracked total passes ~700 MB (Pages caps a published
# site at 1 GB): at that point move the whole folder to an orphan
# `art-archive` branch rather than ignoring it.

# hook scratch: per-session markers for read-the-doc-first
.claude/hooks/.read-state/

# stage_layout_check.mjs writes its contact sheet + screenshots here on every run —
# 142MB of transient probe output per pass; evidence lives in the gate's log and
# the commit messages, never in the repo
layout-check-shots/

# Chrome/Chromium profiles from playtest runs. NEVER commit a --user-data-dir.
# On 2026-08-26, commit 5d82213 added 15 of these (10,713 files, 142 MB) because a
# playtest pointed --user-data-dir INSIDE the repo instead of /tmp. GitHub's secret
# scanner then fired on Default/shared_proto_db/000003.log — it had found the Google
# API key that is baked into every copy of Chrome, sent as X-Goog-Api-Key to
# optimizationguide-pa.googleapis.com. That key is Google's, not ours, so there was
# nothing to rotate; the cost was 142 MB in a public repo and a 3:50am alert email.
# A profile also carries Cookies, Login Data and History — these were empty because
# the profiles were freshly made, which is luck, not a guarantee. See
# docs/DRIVING-THE-GAME.md §8a: user-data-dir belongs in /tmp.
prof-*/
chrome-probe*/
